<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/metaphysics">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson opens the "atlas extension": the part of the course where philosophy turns
    from its core skills (argument, knowledge, ethics) to survey the wider territory. You
    need nothing but what the first lessons built — the habit of finding an argument's
    premises and conclusion — and a tolerance for questions that stay open. Everything else
    is constructed here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what metaphysics and its inventory-taking subfield, ontology, actually study — and what they are not;</li>
    <li>reconstruct Descartes' one undoubtable claim, and the mind–body options (dualism, materialism, idealism) with their signature problem;</li>
    <li>state the problem of universals and the three classic answers to it;</li>
    <li>argue both sides of presentism versus eternalism, including what relativity contributes;</li>
    <li>explain what makes claims about possibility true or false, and what David Lewis proposed;</li>
    <li>summarise the driving question of each of the four extensions: political philosophy, philosophy of science, Eastern philosophy and aesthetics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit throughout: keep two questions apart — <em>what exists?</em> (metaphysics)
    and <em>how do we know?</em> (epistemology, the earlier lessons' business). They lean on
    each other constantly, and confusing them is the most common way to lose an argument you
    were winning.
  </p>

  <h2>The most misused word in philosophy</h2>
  <p>
    Type "metaphysics" into a bookshop search and you will find crystals, tarot decks and
    pyramid power. Philosophers mean something much stricter. <strong>Metaphysics</strong>
    is the study of reality at the most general level; its inventory-taking subfield,
    <strong>ontology</strong>, asks what exists — the "furniture of the universe". Does God
    exist? Do numbers? Do other people, or only your impressions of them? Is the world
    really coloured, or is colour a side effect of how perceivers like us are built? Is a
    number a thing? Does your childhood exist, or only your memories of it?
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this is mysticism. These are rigorous arguments about bookkeeping — the
    ontological ledger of what the world contains — and their answers quietly underwrite
    physics, mathematics, law and every other discipline's implicit picture of reality.
    The widget below is the lesson's map: four classic questions, each with named
    positions, each still open.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four classic questions — existence, universals, time, possibility" widget="equationStep" config={metaCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps are the lesson's spine, and each pairs
    a question with named rival answers — never a vagueness. Step 1 asks what kinds of
    things exist (this section and the two after it), step 2 whether properties exist, step
    3 whether the future exists, step 4 what makes possibility talk true. Not one of the
    four is settled, and that is not a scandal: in metaphysics, an open question with sharp
    positions <em>is</em> progress.
  </p>

  <h2>The one thing you cannot doubt</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the subject's deepest worry, stated in one line: how do you know the real
    world resembles the world you perceive? Look at a chair. What you actually have is a
    picture of a chair <em>in your mind</em> — the chair out there is a conclusion, not an
    observation. Kant gave the two sides names: the mind-side pictures are
    <strong>phenomena</strong>, the things-in-themselves are <strong>noumena</strong>. And
    dreams and hallucinations prove the gap is real: in them you have phenomena with no
    noumenon behind them. So what justifies the usual step from inner picture to outer
    thing?
  </p>
  <p>
    René Descartes, working through this in the 1640s, found one claim that survives every
    doubt: that he existed. Try to doubt your own existence — the doubting is itself
    thinking, and thinking requires a thinker. "I think, therefore I am." It is a tiny
    foundation — notice it establishes only that you exist as a thinking thing, nothing
    about your body or the chair — but it is absolutely secure, and the next four centuries
    of philosophy are built on and around it. (The course returns to Descartes' project in
    full in <em>Reason as a Source of Knowledge</em>.)
  </p>

  <h2>What kinds of stuff exist? Mind, body and the interaction problem</h2>
  <p>
    What kind of thing did Descartes find when he found himself? The common-sense answer,
    shared by most people and many philosophers, is <strong>dualism</strong>: reality
    contains two basic kinds of stuff, mind-stuff and body-stuff — and you are a compound
    of both. Picture a burrito in your mind's eye. The idea is real (it exists as mind
    stuff), but it is not a physical burrito. Two categories, two kinds of being.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trouble is <strong>the problem of interaction</strong>. If mind and body are
    different kinds of stuff, how do they affect each other? To move a chair you must push
    it — physical change needs physical force, and no one can move a chair by thinking at
    it. Yet a mere desire in your mind moves your arm. And it runs the other way: when you
    stub your toe, a perfectly ordinary body-on-body collision somehow produces pain, an
    event with no size, shape or mass. How? Dualism has no universally accepted answer.
    Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed exactly this question on Descartes in their
    correspondence, and philosophers have been pressing it ever since.
  </p>
  <p>
    So many philosophers retreat to <strong>monism</strong> — reality is made of one kind
    of stuff — which splits into two great camps. <strong>Materialism</strong> says only
    physical stuff exists; its hard case is consciousness, because brain states and
    experiences are perfectly correlated (that bit of the brain lights up when the toe
    hurts) and yet the felt pain seems like a different sort of thing from neurons firing.
    <strong>Idealism</strong> says only mind exists — some versions posit a single cosmic
    mind, others many minds and their experiences; the study of experience-as-had became
    the school called phenomenology. The argument between the camps is alive, unresolved,
    and arguably the deepest question in the subject.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "science has settled that the mind just is the brain."
  It seems reasonable — neuroscience finds correlations everywhere, and every mental event
  so far examined has a brain event attached. But correlation is not identity, and the
  hard question stands: why is there something it <em>feels like</em> at all? Materialists
  owe an account of how felt experience relates to brain states; dualists owe an account
  of interaction; idealists owe an account of physics. The mind–body problem is not
  closed; it is the open door to the later philosophy-of-mind lessons.</p>

  <h2>Properties: the problem of universals</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest metaphysical problem on the books is <strong>the problem of
    universals</strong>. Two red apples share something — redness — and science talks of
    nothing else: electrons share charge, atoms share structure. Does the shared property
    <em>exist</em>, and where? <strong>Realists</strong> say yes. Plato located universals
    in a timeless realm of Forms, particular red apples "participating" in Redness itself;
    Aristotle relocated them into the things — no redness without red things.
    <strong>Nominalists</strong> say only particulars exist: "red" is a name we apply, a
    resemblance, a set-membership — not a thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hard case is mathematics, where the debate is fiercest today. Numbers behave
    exactly like realist universals — objective, necessary, shared by all their instances
    — while never being found in any experiment. <strong>Platonism about
    mathematics</strong> (numbers really exist, non-physically) versus <strong>nominalism</strong>
    (mathematics is an indispensable fiction) remains genuinely open — and it matters,
    because "is mathematics discovered or invented?" is this question wearing casual
    clothes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if you cannot bump into it, it does not exist." The
  rule feels right — chairs and apples pass the bump test. But electrons cannot be bumped
  into in any everyday sense, and no one doubts them; and the nominalist, pressing the
  same rule against numbers and properties, ends up denying that two apples share
  <em>anything</em> by being red. The correct picture: what exists is not read off from
  the senses but argued for — which is precisely why the arguments exist.</p>

  <h2>Objects: the ship that would not stay the same</h2>
  <p>
    Objects raise the sibling problem: identity through change. The <strong>Ship of
    Theseus</strong> has its planks replaced one by one until none remain — and then the
    old planks are reassembled into a second ship. Which, if either, is the original? The
    honest answer is that persistence through change is genuinely unresolved. The
    positions on offer: <strong>spatiotemporal continuity</strong> (the repaired ship
    stayed put in one continuous history — it is the same); <strong>psychological or
    biological continuity</strong> (for people: memory chains, the living body);
    <strong>four-dimensionalism</strong> (objects are processes in spacetime —
    "stage-worms" — and "same ship" is a relation between stages, not an all-or-nothing
    fact); or <strong>conventionalism</strong> (identity over time is partly our
    stipulation, and the puzzle simply shows the concept's edge).
  </p>
  <p>
    These are not idle puzzles. The same question returns as personal identity (the
    philosophy-of-mind lesson), as legal personhood (are corporations people? might AIs
    be?), and as biological individuality (colonial organisms, half-identical twins). A
    metaphysician who can say what makes the ship the same ship is doing the groundwork
    for all three.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> metaphysics inventories reality; Descartes secured one entry
    (the thinker); dualism, materialism and idealism disagree about the rest; and even
    middling-sized objects — ships, apples — resist a settled answer about what persists.
    Now the two questions that have produced the most twentieth-century ink: time and
    possibility.
  </p>

  <h2>Time: does the future exist?</h2>
  <p>
    Common sense says the present is special: the past <em>was</em>, the future <em>will
    be</em>, only now <em>is</em>. That is <strong>presentism</strong> — only the present
    is real. Its rival, <strong>eternalism</strong>, says past, present and future all
    exist, spread out like a landscape — the "block universe". The modern argument tilts
    physical: relativity's <em>relativity of simultaneity</em> means there is no universal
    "now" — two observers moving relative to each other disagree about which distant
    events are happening "at the same time" — which undermines the metaphysics of a single
    objective present. It pushed many philosophers, and physicists such as Einstein
    himself, toward eternalism; in a famous condolence letter Einstein wrote that "the
    distinction between past, present and future is only a stubbornly persistent
    illusion".
  </p>
  <p>
    Presentists respond that physics describes structure, not ontology, and that
    eternalism makes the future's openness — and with it free will and genuine becoming —
    hard to state. Note the texture of the debate, because it is the texture of
    metaphysics as such: neither side disputes any <em>experiment</em>; they dispute what
    the experiments imply about what exists.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the block universe means everything is fated — if the
  future already exists, my choices do nothing." It seems to follow, but it confuses two
  independent theses. Eternalism is about <em>what exists</em> (all times, equally);
  determinism is about <em>what causes what</em> (one future fixed by the past). They can
  come apart in every combination: an eternalist can insist the future is settled but by
  our own later choices among its causes, and a presentist can be a strict determinist.
  Whether free will survives either picture is a further, separate question — one the
  course takes up on its own.</p>

  <h2>Possibility: what makes "could have" true?</h2>
  <p>
    Everyday reasoning is saturated with modality — <em>could</em>, <em>must</em>,
    <em>might have been</em> — and metaphysics asks what makes such claims true or false.
    David Lewis's answer (1986) is the boldest: <strong>modal realism</strong> —
    possibilities are other <em>concrete</em> worlds, as real as ours, spatiotemporally
    isolated from it. "The coin could have landed tails" means: in some world, it did. The
    elegance (modality becomes simple counting over worlds; modal logic gets clean
    truth-conditions) trades against ontology: an infinity of entire universes, most of
    them not containing you, existing for the sake of semantics. Alternatives:
    <strong>actualism</strong> — possibilities are ways <em>this</em> world could have
    been, built from abstract objects; <strong>fictionalism</strong> — possible worlds are
    a useful make-believe. Where you land shapes modal logic, counterfactual explanations
    in science, and how seriously to take the many-worlds interpretation of quantum
    mechanics — a physics proposal that, unusually, would make something like Lewis's
    ontology empirical.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Lewis's possible worlds are the same thing as the
  quantum multiverse." The two get compared constantly, and the comparison is
  illuminating precisely because it fails. Quantum many-worlds is a claim about
  <em>this</em> universe's dynamics: its branches share one past, evolve by known physical
  law, and (on most readings) cannot communicate. Lewis's worlds are spatiotemporally
  isolated from ours altogether — no shared past, no shared laws even, no possibility of
  contact. One is physics, the other is semantics dressed in ontology; noticing the
  difference is a good test of whether you have understood both.</p>

  <h2>Political philosophy: what justifies the state?</h2>
  <p>
    The atlas's first extension asks where the state's authority comes from.
    <strong>Social contract theory</strong> answers by construction: imagine humans with
    no government and ask what they would rationally agree to. <strong>Hobbes</strong>
    (1651): the state of nature is a "war of all against all" where life is "solitary,
    poor, nasty, brutish, and short" — so self-preservation licenses surrendering nearly
    all freedom to an absolute sovereign. Order first, liberty later.
    <strong>Locke</strong> (1689): the state of nature already has natural rights — life,
    liberty, property — and government is a limited trustee we may <em>replace</em> when
    it betrays the trust; the intellectual blueprint of constitutional democracy.
    <strong>Rousseau</strong> (1762): the contract must unite citizens under the "general
    will" — laws we give ourselves collectively — freedom as self-rule rather than as the
    absence of constraint.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two centuries of refinement sharpened the game: Rawls (1971) re-ran the contract
    behind a "veil of ignorance" — design society without knowing who you will be in it —
    and derived liberal-egalitarian principles; Nozick ran it libertarian; critics (Hume
    first: no one ever <em>signed</em>) press on the fiction of consent. The underwriting,
    though, is real: every constitution is an answer to the contract's question.
  </p>

  <h2>Philosophy of science: what makes science knowledge?</h2>
  <p>
    The second extension asks what gives science its authority. <strong>The problem of
    induction</strong> (Hume, 1739) starts it: no number of confirming observations —
    however many white swans — logically guarantees the next one, so science's basic
    inference cannot be proven without circularity. Responses define the twentieth
    century. <strong>Popper's demarcation</strong>: science is distinguished not by
    <em>proving</em> theories but by making them <em>falsifiable</em> — a theory that
    cannot possibly fail is not science (a criterion aimed polemically at Freud and Marx
    as much as at astrology). <strong>Kuhn's revolutions</strong> (1962): actual science
    runs in "paradigms" — accepted exemplars and methods — that accumulate anomalies until
    a crisis replaces the whole framework; the image of science as steady falsification,
    Kuhn showed, fits the textbook, not the history. Popper and Kuhn still frame every
    argument about pseudoscience, replication and expert trust.
  </p>

  <h2>Eastern philosophy: other traditions, same rigour</h2>
  <p>
    The atlas is wider than the Western canon. <strong>Vedanta</strong> (the closing
    schools of the Vedas) centres on <em>Brahman</em> — ultimate reality — and the
    identity of <em>Atman</em>, the innermost self, with it; Shankara's Advaita ("not
    two") argues that the everyday world of separate selves is <em>maya</em>, appearance —
    a metaphysical thesis argued with the same rigour (and some of the same puzzles) as
    Western idealism. <strong>Buddhism</strong> builds a metaphysics of <em>anatta</em>
    (no permanent self: persons are causally connected processes, the flame passed
    candle to candle) and <em>pratītyasamutpāda</em> (dependent origination: everything
    arises conditioned on other things) — arguments that anticipate four-dimensionalism
    and process philosophy by two millennia, attached to a therapeutic method: meditation
    as investigation. <strong>Zen</strong> distrusts conceptual elaboration itself; its
    koans ("what is the sound of one hand?") are designed to break discursive thought and
    force direct insight — a live philosophical position about the <em>limits</em> of the
    argument method this unit teaches, argued from inside a rival tradition. Together
    with Confucian ethics (role, ritual, relationship) and Daoist naturalism (the
    <em>Dao</em> that "spoken, is not the constant Dao"), they make the point the atlas
    exists to make: the deepest questions have multiple working traditions, and reading
    only one is a smaller map.
  </p>

  <h2>Aesthetics: is beauty in the object?</h2>
  <p>
    The last extension asks whether beauty is in the thing or the eye.
    <strong>Objectivists</strong> (Plato: beauty as a Form; the classical canon of
    proportion) say some things are beautiful full stop; <strong>subjectivists</strong>
    say taste decides — and Hume gave subjectivism its best form: beauty is a sentiment,
    but <em>judges of taste</em> can be trained, so verdicts are not all equal. Kant split
    the difference in the <em>Critique of Judgment</em>: judgements of beauty claim
    universal agreement ("this <em>is</em> beautiful", not "I like it") yet rest on no
    concept — the "free play" of the faculties, disinterested pleasure.
  </p>
  <p>
    The modern debate took its turn after Duchamp exhibited a urinal in 1917: an ordinary
    object, declared art by context and theory. If art cannot be defined by beautiful
    appearance at all, what defines it? The <strong>institutional theory</strong> (Danto,
    Dickie): art is whatever the <em>artworld</em> confers the status of art upon. The
    philosophy keeps its edge wherever practice outruns the definition: AI-generated
    images, found sound, process art — each a test case the objectivists, subjectivists
    and institutionalists grade differently.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> four classic questions about the inventory of reality, and
    four extension provinces where philosophy audits its neighbours. The pattern to carry
    away: in every case the rival positions are sharp, the arguments are real, and the
    questions stay open — which is what mature enquiry looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A bookshop shelves "metaphysics" next to the tarot decks. What do philosophers mean by the word, and what does ontology add?</li>
    <li>State the interaction problem as an objection to dualism.</li>
    <li>Two red apples are both red. Give the realist's answer and the nominalist's answer to what they share.</li>
    <li>What does relativity's relativity of simultaneity do to presentism — and why is that not the end of the argument?</li>
    <li>Give one necessary and one sufficient condition for being a millionaire, in the philosopher's sense of the terms.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Metaphysics is the general study of reality — its most
    basic kinds of things and their nature; ontology is its inventory-taking subfield,
    asking what exists. Neither involves crystals or pyramid power. (2) If mind and body
    are wholly different kinds of substance, neither should be able to move the other:
    physical change needs physical force, yet desires move arms and collisions produce
    pain. Since interaction plainly happens, dualism owes an account of how. (3) Realist:
    they share a real property — redness — either in Plato's realm of Forms or (Aristotle)
    in the apples themselves. Nominalist: only the particulars exist; "red" is a name for
    their resemblance, not a third thing. (4) If observers in relative motion disagree
    about what is happening "now" elsewhere, there is no single objective present for
    presentism to privilege — this pushes many toward eternalism. But it is not decisive:
    presentists reply that physics describes structure, not what exists, and the debate
    over that reply continues. (5) Necessary: having a million dollars (or equivalent
    wealth) — no millionaire lacks it. Sufficient: inheriting a million dollars — by
    itself it makes you a millionaire, though you could get there other ways, by winning a
    lottery or building a company. A condition you must have is necessary; a condition
    that alone gets you in is sufficient; a definition is a condition set that is both.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Metaphysics is the study of reality at the most general level; ontology is its inventory — the furniture of the universe. It is not the crystals-and-tarot sense.</li>
    <li>Kant's phenomena/noumena gap and Descartes' cogito ("I think, therefore I am") frame the founding question: what justifies the step from inner picture to outer world?</li>
    <li>Mind and body: dualism faces the interaction problem; monism splits into materialism (hard case: consciousness) and idealism. All three are live.</li>
    <li>Universals: realists (Plato's Forms, or Aristotle's properties-in-things) versus nominalists; mathematics is the hardest case — discovered or invented?</li>
    <li>Identity through change (Ship of Theseus) has four live answers: continuity, four-dimensionalism, psychological continuity, conventionalism.</li>
    <li>Time: presentism (only the present real) versus eternalism (the block universe), with relativity's no-universal-now favouring eternalism — but no experiment settles it.</li>
    <li>Possibility: Lewis's modal realism (concrete other worlds) versus actualism and fictionalism; modal logic, science's counterfactuals and quantum many-worlds all inherit the choice.</li>
    <li>The four extensions share one spine: each asks where a practice's authority comes from — the state's, science's, the tradition's, the artworld's — and finds the answer in agreement, testability, insight or conferred status, not raw force or raw fact.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Every province on this map has its own intermediate lesson: <em>Political
    Philosophy</em> takes the contract from Hobbes to Rawls in full; <em>Philosophy of
    Science</em> works through induction, Popper and Kuhn with the case studies;
    <em>Eastern Philosophy</em> gives Vedanta, Buddhism and Zen the close reading they
    were abbreviated to here; <em>Aesthetics</em> takes the art-argument from Kant to the
    artworld. Closer to home, the mind–body problem you met today is the doorway to
    <em>Philosophy of Mind</em> — dualist theories, physicalism and functionalism — and
    <em>Reason as a Source of Knowledge</em> retraces Descartes' whole project properly.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer of metaphysics is its <strong>method</strong>: without experiments,
    the field works by systematic inference to the best explanation over our total
    commitments (Quine's "web of belief"), by conceptual engineering — sharpening or
    revising the concepts themselves — and by controlled intuition-pumping through thought
    experiments (the ship, the teleporter, the twin Earth). One tool from that kit deserves
    its own paragraph, because you will use it in every lesson that follows. Metaphysicians
    (and philosophers generally) try to define a concept with <strong>necessary and
    sufficient conditions</strong>: a necessary condition is one every member of the group
    must have (a pulse, for a living human); a sufficient condition is one that alone gets
    you in (inheriting a million dollars, for being a millionaire). A definition that
    requires too much is too <em>narrow</em> — "featherless biped" fails because a person
    who loses a leg is still human; one that requires too little is too <em>broad</em> —
    "a thing people sit on" fails because we sit on floors, which are not chairs. A good
    definition is exactly as broad as the group and no broader — and testing candidate
    definitions against edge cases is half of what philosophical argument is.
  </p>
  <p>
    Physics increasingly sets the boundary conditions: relativity constrains time
    metaphysics; quantum mechanics revives the question of what makes an outcome actual
    (the measurement problem <em>is</em> a metaphysical question with laboratory stakes);
    thermodynamics grounds the arrow of time in entropy. And ontology's hardest open case
    remains mathematics: Benacerraf's dilemma — if numbers exist outside space and time,
    how do we know them; if they do not, why is mathematics objectively true? — has no
    consensus answer after fifty years. The inventory is still open: numbers, possible
    worlds, the present moment, the self. Whether those exist, and in what sense, are live
    questions argued in journals this year; the widget's four questions are not history —
    they are the current frontier's front door.
  </p>

